    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d180">

                <pb facs="0514" n="480" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v09/pg480"/>

                <head>180. Telegram From the Delegation at the Summit Conference to the Department
                    of State<anchor n="0" xml:id="d180-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Paris</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1960-05-17T00:00:00+01:00">May 17, 1960,
                            midnight</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Secto 26. At meeting of three Western Heads of Government, 9:30 p.m. May 17 at
                    Elysee Palace,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d180-f2"/>De Gaulle reported that he had
                    been informed Khrushchev would pay him final call at 11 Wednesday morning, hold
                    press conference 3 p.m.,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d180-f3"/> and depart Paris 11
                    Thursday morning.</p>
                <p>De Gaulle said question of tripartite communiqué (text telegraphed separately
                    Exhagerty four)<anchor n="3" xml:id="d180-f4"/> remained to be agreed. This was
                    done promptly and issued 10 tonight Paris time.</p>
                <pb facs="0515" n="481" xml:id="pg481"/>
                <p>In response to inquiry, President said he intends to depart Paris Thursday
                    morning paying morning farewell call on De Gaulle prior departure.</p>
                <p>It was further agreed three Western Foreign Ministers would meet at Quai d’Orsay
                    at 11 a.m. May 18, and that three Western Heads of Government would meet at
                    Elysee at 5 p.m. for final consideration subjects on summit agenda and situation
                    arising from its failure to convene.</p>
                <p>De Gaulle attended by Debre and Couve de Murville; Macmillan by Lloyd, Hoyer
                    Millar and Rumbold; and the President by the Secretary, Secretary Gates,
                    Whitney, Houghton and Merchant.</p>
                <p>Macmillan stated he planned to depart for London Thursday morning. Lloyd reported
                    on talk with Gromyko before dinner as reported separately.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d180-f5"/></p>
                <p>Following meeting French said Khrushchev’s evening letter to De Gaulle re
                    non-attendance summit meeting this afternoon and De Gaulle’s succinct reply were
                    being released to press.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d180-f6"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Herter</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d180-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d180-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 396.1–PA/5–1760. Secret; Priority.
                    Repeated to London, Bonn, and Moscow.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d180-f2" n="1" xml:id="d180-f2-content">
                <p>For another brief account of this meeting, see Macmillan, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Pointing the Way</hi>, p. 211.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d180-f3" n="2" xml:id="d180-f3-content">
                <p>For a transcript of Khrushchev’s press conference, see <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Background Documents</hi>, pp. 56–68.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d180-f4" n="3" xml:id="d180-f4-content">
                <p>For text of the communiqué, see <hi rendition="#italic">American Foreign Policy:
                        Current Documents, 1960</hi>, p. 431. The Exhagerty four cable has not been
                    found.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d180-f5" n="4" xml:id="d180-f5-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d179">Document 179</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d180-f6" n="5" xml:id="d180-f6-content">
                <p>Following this meeting, Kohler briefed Grewe on its substance and on the
                    substance of Lloyd’s conversation with Gromyko. A memorandum of their
                    conversation (US/MC/21) is in Department of State, Conference Files: Lot 64 D
                    559, CF 1664.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
